# 6. PROBLEM STATEMENT ALIGNMENT

The original competition problem is:

Build a GenAI-powered university laboratory resource-sharing platform
that enables students and faculty to discover specialized equipment,
dev kits, laboratory workstations and resources across departments.

The platform should support:

- Cross-department resource discovery.
- Intelligent equipment matching.
- Project-based access requests.
- Slot booking.
- Faculty approvals.
- Fair and feasible conflict resolution.
- Deadline-aware scheduling.
- Optimization of resource utilization.
- Better access to specialized resources.
- Practical collaboration between departments.

Map every requirement to actual implemented functionality.

Verify:

## Discovery
- Resources can be discovered across departments.
- Resource details are useful and accurate.
- Filters are functional.
- Availability information is trustworthy.

## Intelligent matching
- Projects are matched to relevant equipment.
- Matching considers real resource specifications.
- Matching results are explainable.
- AI-generated recommendations are validated.

## Project-based requests
- Requests capture meaningful project information.
- Requests identify actual resources.
- Request workflows are functional.

## Booking
- Booking is functional end-to-end.
- Booking eligibility is enforced.
- Booking conflicts are prevented.
- Booking status is accurate.

## Faculty approval
- Approval workflows are functional.
- Approvals are authorized.
- Approval decisions are recorded.
- Approval status is reflected correctly.

## Fair allocation
- Conflicts are resolved using a meaningful algorithm.
- Decisions are explainable.
- Deadlines are considered.
- Suitability is considered.
- Fairness is considered.
- Resource availability is considered.
- Alternatives are considered when appropriate.

## Scheduling
- Scheduling respects real availability.
- Scheduling respects resource constraints.
- Scheduling does not double-book.
- Scheduling identifies feasible alternatives.

## Resource utilization
- Metrics are computed from actual data.
- Metrics are not hardcoded or misleading.
- Utilization improvements are demonstrable.

## AI functionality
- AI genuinely adds value.
- AI is not merely decorative.
- AI does not fabricate live information.
- AI is not responsible for security-critical decisions.

For every requirement:

- Identify its actual implementation.
- Identify the exact files involved.
- Determine whether it is fully implemented, partially implemented,
  missing or unverifiable.
- Identify weaknesses.
- Propose concrete fixes.

Detect features that are irrelevant, overengineered, distracting,
or do not contribute to the problem statement.

